```\n\nWait — the column order must be `cell_id,n_timepoints,mean_distance_nm,contact_fraction,transcription_fraction`. Let me reorder.\n\nLag analysis: for each lag k:\n- For each cell (grouped), valid t indices: t such that t and t+lag are within the cell's timepoints. To be safe, sort by time within cell and use position-based alignment. Times appear to be contiguous integers from 0 to 249. I'll sort by time within cell and verify contiguity.\n\nPooled correlation: concatenate contact[t] and transcription[t+lag] across all cells and compute Pearson r with np.corrcoef.\n\nn_observations = total number of pairs.\n\nImplementation approach: pivot to per-cell arrays. With 600 cells × 250, a numpy 2D array of shape (600, 250) is fine.\n\n```python\ncells = sorted(df.cell_id.unique())\n# build matrices\ncontact_mat = np.full((len(cells), max_t), np.nan)\ntrans_mat = ...\n```\n\nOr more simply: assume each cell has exactly 250 consecutive timepoints from 0 to 249 (verify with assertion). Then pivot:\n\n```python\ncontact = df.pivot(index=\"cell_id\", columns=\"time\", values=\"contact\").to_numpy()\ntrans = df.pivot(index=\"cell_id\", columns=\"time\", values=\"transcription\").to_numpy()\nT = contact.shape[1]\nrows = []\nfor lag in range(-20, 21):\n    if lag >= 0:\n        c = contact[:, :T-lag].ravel()\n        tr = trans[:, lag:].ravel()\n    else:\n        c = contact[:, -lag:].ravel()\n        tr = trans[:, :T+lag].ravel()\n    mask = ~(np.isnan(c) | np.isnan(tr))  # if int, no NaNs\n    r = np.corrcoef(c[mask], tr[mask])[0,1]\n    rows.append((lag, r, int(mask.sum())))\n```\n\nNote that pandas 3.0 pivot requires unique index/column pairs — no problem if data is well-formed.\n\nOne subtle point: \"pooled Pearson correlation across all cells\" could also be interpreted as a cell-wise mean of correlations. The literal reading of the rule is pooling. I'll implement the pooled version as primary. I can mention both in the report and include a sensitivity analysis, but