{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting results:\n- 600 cells, 250 timepoints each, 150,000 rows.\n- Overall average distance ~526 nm; contact fraction (≤260 nm) ~5.34%; transcription fraction ~15.4%.\n- Lag analysis: strongest pooled |association| is lag = -1 (r = 0.0778). Second is lag = +1 (r = 0.0316). Everything else is near 0 (|r| < 0.007).\n\nNote on lag sign convention: positive lag means contact leads transcription (transcription at time t+lag). So lag = -1 means contact at time t is correlated with transcription at time t-1, i.e., transcription at the previous timepoint predicts contact at the next timepoint — transcription leads contact by 1 step. lag = +1 means contact at time t predicts transcription at time t+1 — contact leads transcription by 1 step.\n\nInteresting point: the strongest association is lag=-1 (transcription leads contact), with contact leading at lag=+1 being weaker. Both are positive correlations. The rule says \"the largest absolute association is to be interpreted only as a temporal association, not as proof of causation.\" So the report should state that the maximum is at lag=-1, r≈0.078, n=149,400.\n\nAlso worth noting: the contemporaneous correlation (lag 0) is essentially 0. And the within-cell sensitivity analysis agrees (lag=-1 is strongest, r≈0.074), so the pooled estimate isn't just an artifact of between-cell confounding.\n\nNow let me verify that the output CSVs look right (headers, format). Then I'll write report.md.\n\nCheck lag_analysis.csv format: association values are printed with full precision. Let me look at the actual file."}}
